WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.709 --> 00:00:08.129
My very first food memory
was when I was three or four.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:09.881
It was a warm day,

00:00:09.881 --> 00:00:13.260
and my mom had just picked me
and my sister up from school,

00:00:13.301 --> 00:00:17.305
buckled us into the back
of her dusty blue Ford Taurus.

00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:20.225
She was halfway out
of the school parking lot

00:00:20.225 --> 00:00:21.810
when she pulled over to the side.

00:00:22.227 --> 00:00:25.939
She strangely reached for her purse
that was resting on the dashboard,

00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:28.650
started fishing around for something
until she found it.

00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:31.444
Then she twisted around to us,

00:00:31.444 --> 00:00:34.781
and she tore open a yellow paper bag

00:00:34.823 --> 00:00:37.826
of slightly melted Sugar Babies.

00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:39.703
(Audience murmurs)

00:00:39.744 --> 00:00:42.706
She poured a few of the pellets
into each of our tiny hands.

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:44.332
Now, I want to be clear,

00:00:44.332 --> 00:00:46.751
this was not a normal mom thing.

00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:48.878
This was breaking several rules.

00:00:49.337 --> 00:00:51.756
It was after lunch but before dinner,

00:00:51.756 --> 00:00:53.300
we were in the car,

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.551
(Laughs)

00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:57.470
we hadn't done anything spectacular
to be celebrated.

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:01.099
And Sugar Babies are, well, pure sugar.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:03.893
I was so confused.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.981
But I was a kid, so I took the candy,
and I started chewing.

00:01:08.815 --> 00:01:13.570
God, I felt like I had just been inducted
into the coolest club possible.

00:01:14.112 --> 00:01:16.698
The one that eats candy
on a random Wednesday.

00:01:16.698 --> 00:01:18.158
(Laughs)

00:01:18.199 --> 00:01:22.329
And then the world, it slowed down.

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:24.414
Everything faded.

00:01:25.373 --> 00:01:27.125
And then everything sharpened.

00:01:28.084 --> 00:01:31.338
There is only one word
for what I felt in that moment.

00:01:31.713 --> 00:01:32.964
Magic.

00:01:33.006 --> 00:01:34.716
Real magic.

00:01:35.842 --> 00:01:38.887
Now, most people think
that’s kind of crazy,

00:01:38.928 --> 00:01:40.138
three or four years old.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.808
I don't remember anything
about my childhood for years after that,

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.685
but I remember that day,

00:01:45.685 --> 00:01:48.063
I remember those Sugar Babies,

00:01:48.104 --> 00:01:49.981
and I remember that feeling.

00:01:51.232 --> 00:01:55.570
Now, most people think of magic
as a trick, right?

00:01:55.612 --> 00:01:56.738
Abracadabra.

00:01:57.113 --> 00:02:01.159
This seemingly impossible act,
put on purely for entertainment.

00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:03.495
But I'd like to challenge that.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.874
Magic, real magic is not an illusion.

00:02:09.125 --> 00:02:11.628
Real magic is the feeling

00:02:11.628 --> 00:02:15.423
when life transforms
from the ordinary into the extraordinary.

00:02:17.425 --> 00:02:19.552
And not only is it real.

00:02:19.594 --> 00:02:23.264
It is the very thing
that connects us to this world,

00:02:23.306 --> 00:02:25.058
to one another,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.768
to our entire existence.

00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:29.646
It's the spark of a moment.

00:02:30.063 --> 00:02:32.732
It's the feeling of believing,

00:02:32.774 --> 00:02:34.192
of belonging,

00:02:34.234 --> 00:02:35.443
of getting,

00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:36.945
of being gotten.

00:02:38.196 --> 00:02:41.700
It is the crack

00:02:41.741 --> 00:02:46.413
that opens up our hearts
and reminds us to let light in.

00:02:48.498 --> 00:02:51.167
Now I own Milk Bar,

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:53.461
this quirky American-style bakery

00:02:53.461 --> 00:02:55.588
that I'll tell you
a little bit about later.

00:02:55.588 --> 00:02:57.340
And I have found

00:02:57.382 --> 00:03:01.010
that magic is often created
by breaking some sort of rule.

00:03:02.053 --> 00:03:04.347
Like the "no sweets before dinner" rule.

00:03:04.389 --> 00:03:07.934
Or wearing overalls
to a corporate board meeting.

00:03:08.935 --> 00:03:14.232
Or simply daring to defy the expectations
of the people around us.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.568
Now, as a kid,

00:03:16.568 --> 00:03:19.320
breaking the rules usually
only got us in trouble, right?

00:03:19.738 --> 00:03:22.490
But as adults, it's a different story.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.786
Breaking the rules, pushing boundaries,

00:03:26.828 --> 00:03:29.539
challenging the norm,

00:03:29.581 --> 00:03:32.292
asking why, why not?

00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:34.294
It does something to us.

00:03:34.961 --> 00:03:37.672
It shakes us from sleepwalking.

00:03:38.631 --> 00:03:42.177
And it makes space for magic to sneak in.

00:03:44.345 --> 00:03:47.140
Now I was raised by matriarchs,

00:03:47.182 --> 00:03:51.144
fierce heroic grandmas
who loved to bake in their free time.

00:03:51.686 --> 00:03:56.775
And they knew good and well that food
was for nutrition and sustenance,

00:03:56.775 --> 00:03:58.026
but they didn't care.

00:03:58.067 --> 00:03:59.778
(Laughs)

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:05.033
They'd baked as their way
of breaking the rules.

00:04:05.074 --> 00:04:09.829
They'd make batches of lemon bars
instead of casseroles

00:04:09.829 --> 00:04:11.915
or one-pot wonders.

00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:14.459
And they put those baked goods in baggies

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:15.668
and hand them out,

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.505
hand that magic out
to the most unassuming people.

00:04:18.505 --> 00:04:21.007
The newcomer to the neighborhood,

00:04:21.007 --> 00:04:23.009
the mail carrier or my personal favorite,

00:04:23.051 --> 00:04:25.178
the receptionist at the dentist office.

00:04:25.178 --> 00:04:26.971
(Laughs)

00:04:27.013 --> 00:04:31.935
Now I knew what the receiving end
of magic felt like

00:04:31.976 --> 00:04:33.603
from that Sugar Baby moment.

00:04:34.187 --> 00:04:39.818
But witnessing what the giving part
of real magic did ...

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:42.904
It moved these people.

00:04:43.279 --> 00:04:45.615
It was awe-inspiring.

00:04:46.366 --> 00:04:48.117
And so I decided

00:04:48.117 --> 00:04:51.996
that baking would be my way
of making magic in the world,

00:04:52.038 --> 00:04:54.999
creating a moment and then giving it away.

00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:57.752
A magic pyramid scheme, basically,

00:04:57.752 --> 00:05:01.172
that I wanted, needed more of.

00:05:01.756 --> 00:05:06.010
Only my mom and my grandmas
thought that baking was just a hobby.

00:05:06.678 --> 00:05:08.596
But I knew it could be more than that.

00:05:08.972 --> 00:05:13.309
So I moved to New York City
to become a professional pastry chef.

00:05:14.143 --> 00:05:15.687
I went to culinary school;

00:05:15.728 --> 00:05:18.523
I climbed the ladder of fancy restaurants,

00:05:18.523 --> 00:05:22.944
making fancy desserts
to become the very best of the best.

00:05:23.862 --> 00:05:25.488
Only ...

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:30.618
I never found magic in those beautiful,
delicate plated desserts.

00:05:32.495 --> 00:05:37.500
I could only find my magic
in a cookie or a slice of cake

00:05:37.542 --> 00:05:39.294
or a gooey underbaked pie.

00:05:42.297 --> 00:05:44.007
And so when I opened Milk Bar,

00:05:44.007 --> 00:05:46.509
that bakery that I was telling you about,

00:05:46.551 --> 00:05:51.681
I decided I wasn't going to frost
the sides of a cake

00:05:51.723 --> 00:05:53.474
like the textbooks taught me.

00:05:53.474 --> 00:05:54.809
(Laughter)

00:05:55.685 --> 00:05:59.314
I decided I was going to load
cookie dough with marshmallows

00:05:59.314 --> 00:06:01.274
or pretzels and potato chips

00:06:01.274 --> 00:06:02.942
and butterscotch chips and coffee,

00:06:02.942 --> 00:06:05.194
tossing convention out the window.

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:09.949
I wanted people to eat birthday cake,
not just on their birthday,

00:06:09.991 --> 00:06:11.826
but any day they wanted.

00:06:14.287 --> 00:06:19.167
I knew that my magic came in the form
of these simple baked goods,

00:06:19.208 --> 00:06:23.922
but I also knew that simple,
approachable, accessible,

00:06:23.963 --> 00:06:26.215
nostalgic flavors

00:06:26.257 --> 00:06:29.636
were my best jumping-off place
to create new desserts.

00:06:30.303 --> 00:06:34.432
Almost as though fancy
were the enemy of delicious.

00:06:35.558 --> 00:06:38.186
I’d go to county fairs to nosh.

00:06:38.227 --> 00:06:40.772
I'd go to the diner
and order slices of pie

00:06:40.772 --> 00:06:42.982
from that revolving display case.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:48.279
I'd take french fries and dip them
in chocolate milkshake and dream.

00:06:49.322 --> 00:06:53.117
And then I'd go into the kitchen
and tie on my apron

00:06:53.117 --> 00:06:56.621
and start mixing up a new creation

00:06:56.621 --> 00:07:00.583
rooted in something known
and safe and loved

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.129
but reaching far beyond
what anyone thought accessible.

00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:07.632
Or approachable.

00:07:09.175 --> 00:07:10.468
Or doable.

00:07:11.761 --> 00:07:15.848
Breaking the banking rules
became my daily ritual.

00:07:17.475 --> 00:07:22.605
I mean, who says a layer cake can't taste
like salty malty sweet pretzels?

00:07:23.398 --> 00:07:25.316
I mean, my culinary instructors for one.

00:07:25.358 --> 00:07:26.526
(Laughs)

00:07:26.526 --> 00:07:28.194
"Watch me," I'd think.

00:07:28.194 --> 00:07:29.362
And I'd take pretzels,

00:07:29.404 --> 00:07:31.864
toast them in the oven
to deepen their flavor,

00:07:31.906 --> 00:07:35.493
then grind them
into a powder-like consistency

00:07:35.535 --> 00:07:37.954
and substitute some
of the cake flour in a recipe

00:07:37.954 --> 00:07:39.455
for that pretzel powder.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:41.708
It's pretty good.

00:07:43.376 --> 00:07:47.213
When I was opening Milk Bar,
I knew I wanted to serve ice cream.

00:07:47.255 --> 00:07:49.257
A rebellious kind of ice cream.

00:07:49.298 --> 00:07:50.925
No surprise, right?

00:07:50.967 --> 00:07:53.344
So I decided to buy a soft serve machine,

00:07:53.386 --> 00:07:56.222
which for the record,
was very rebellious at the time.

00:07:57.265 --> 00:07:59.308
I had to decide what flavor to make.

00:08:00.893 --> 00:08:04.772
I mean, why do we only accept
chocolate and vanilla

00:08:04.772 --> 00:08:06.733
as ice cream flavors?

00:08:08.943 --> 00:08:11.779
I needed to come up
with something better, different.

00:08:12.488 --> 00:08:13.990
Push that boundary.

00:08:15.241 --> 00:08:18.745
I knew I had to come up
with a flavor of milk

00:08:18.745 --> 00:08:19.954
that was different.

00:08:19.996 --> 00:08:22.415
Milk is, like, the base
of any great ice cream.

00:08:23.291 --> 00:08:27.336
So I sat out inspiration
at my 24-hour bodega.

00:08:27.336 --> 00:08:28.755
Favorite place to go.

00:08:29.422 --> 00:08:31.007
Because that's where we all shop,

00:08:31.049 --> 00:08:33.217
that's where our simple
taste buds are formed,

00:08:33.259 --> 00:08:35.053
it's the flavors we know.

00:08:35.845 --> 00:08:38.806
Up and down the aisles till I hit it.

00:08:39.265 --> 00:08:42.518
My favorite section
of the grocery store as a kid,

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:43.728
the cereal aisle.

00:08:45.021 --> 00:08:47.148
Now as a kid, my mom and I
had an agreement.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.649
I was a pretty picky eater,

00:08:48.691 --> 00:08:52.195
and we agreed that I could have
as much cereal as I wanted

00:08:52.195 --> 00:08:54.614
as long as I drank
all the calcium-rich milk

00:08:54.614 --> 00:08:56.365
at the bottom of the bowl.

00:08:56.741 --> 00:08:58.910
I mean, I thought it was highway robbery.

00:08:58.951 --> 00:09:03.623
Does she not understand how good
that sugary sweet milk was at the bottom?

00:09:04.749 --> 00:09:06.125
That's interesting.

00:09:07.168 --> 00:09:09.420
That's a really delicious flavor of milk.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:10.838
It moved me.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:12.840
It made sense to me,

00:09:12.882 --> 00:09:15.218
but would it make sense to other people?

00:09:16.385 --> 00:09:17.845
Figured I'd give it a try.

00:09:18.721 --> 00:09:20.139
So I went back to the kitchen

00:09:20.139 --> 00:09:22.475
and made the equivalent
of a giant bowl of cereal.

00:09:22.517 --> 00:09:25.144
I strained out all that delicious milk,

00:09:25.186 --> 00:09:27.105
and I spun it into ice cream.

00:09:27.522 --> 00:09:28.689
Put it on the menu.

00:09:30.149 --> 00:09:31.943
And I've got to tell you, even now,

00:09:31.984 --> 00:09:33.903
people stop me on the street

00:09:33.945 --> 00:09:37.406
to tell me about their cereal-milk
ice cream moment.

00:09:38.366 --> 00:09:39.534
"So good."

00:09:39.534 --> 00:09:40.993
"Seemingly a little naughty."

00:09:41.035 --> 00:09:43.162
I mean, how did I know
that was their flavor?

00:09:43.162 --> 00:09:45.790
That's the flavor they eat
when they're wearing pajamas,

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:46.999
when no one's looking.

00:09:47.041 --> 00:09:48.501
The flavor of their childhood.

00:09:48.543 --> 00:09:52.505
These people will tell me
about every detail of the day.

00:09:52.505 --> 00:09:56.384
They will tell me about the weather,
the company they kept,

00:09:56.425 --> 00:10:01.430
the way that an ordinary moment
was transformed into something magical

00:10:01.472 --> 00:10:03.182
with a simple bite.

00:10:06.602 --> 00:10:10.314
I built my business with the same
"challenge the norm" mentality.

00:10:12.733 --> 00:10:17.113
Rather than measure my business's success
by the profit or loss sheet,

00:10:17.155 --> 00:10:18.656
by the average order value,

00:10:18.698 --> 00:10:22.368
the other metrics that businesses use

00:10:22.368 --> 00:10:24.370
to know if they're doing well,

00:10:24.412 --> 00:10:29.375
I decided we'd measure our impact
by the twinkle in people's eyes.

00:10:29.876 --> 00:10:33.045
By the "Oh, I can't put that down" feeling

00:10:33.087 --> 00:10:36.090
that they had when they ate
one of our desserts.

00:10:36.841 --> 00:10:41.220
I chose to put women
at the helm of my organization.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.098
Because when I looked
around the hospitality industry,

00:10:44.098 --> 00:10:48.603
people running operations,
folks holding CEO titles,

00:10:48.644 --> 00:10:49.937
they were all men.

00:10:50.646 --> 00:10:51.898
I mean, why?

00:10:51.939 --> 00:10:56.444
I was raised by these fierce matriarchs
who handled business,

00:10:56.485 --> 00:10:58.905
and I wanted my organization
to look like that.

00:10:58.946 --> 00:11:02.658
I want to show the world
what women were capable of

00:11:02.658 --> 00:11:04.660
and what a "bakery" could be.

00:11:04.994 --> 00:11:07.955
Push back on that cute little box
people like to put us in.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:12.835
And in turn, the rules broke for us.

00:11:13.794 --> 00:11:17.715
The magic, our magic, got out.

00:11:18.424 --> 00:11:21.219
Word spread without us spending
a dollar on marketing.

00:11:21.677 --> 00:11:23.137
Rare in our industry,

00:11:23.179 --> 00:11:26.057
we grew our business
without diluting the business.

00:11:26.641 --> 00:11:27.934
I mean, we won awards

00:11:27.975 --> 00:11:31.562
no tiny East Village bakery
has any business winning.

00:11:31.604 --> 00:11:32.730
(Laughs)

00:11:33.147 --> 00:11:34.565
It's pretty incredible.

00:11:35.316 --> 00:11:37.568
Even today

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:40.404
I get to witness that feeling,

00:11:40.446 --> 00:11:43.908
that same one that my grandmas
conjured up in my childhood.

00:11:45.076 --> 00:11:47.328
We ship cakes all across the country,

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.831
we keep our doors open from early
in the morning until late at night.

00:11:50.831 --> 00:11:53.125
It's insane for a bakery to do.

00:11:53.459 --> 00:11:56.003
But we do it because we're
obsessed with it.

00:11:56.045 --> 00:11:57.713
We're obsessed with what we do.

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:01.509
We teach classes, we share recipes

00:12:01.509 --> 00:12:04.345
despite any concerns of copycats

00:12:04.387 --> 00:12:06.973
or intellectual property infringements,

00:12:07.014 --> 00:12:09.642
because that's what you do

00:12:09.642 --> 00:12:12.478
when you benefit
from what came before you

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:15.648
and you plan on passing it
along long after you.

00:12:16.816 --> 00:12:17.984
Thank you.

00:12:18.025 --> 00:12:21.279
(Applause)

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.448
When you know you're here
to give, not take,

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:27.326
and leave this place
better than you found it.

00:12:29.453 --> 00:12:32.331
See, mom, you can bake for a living.

00:12:32.331 --> 00:12:33.916
Pretty cool, if you ask me.

00:12:34.834 --> 00:12:37.378
Now I don't have
the full equation cracked,

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:41.882
but I do know that it starts
with the decision to act.

00:12:42.842 --> 00:12:46.554
My mom, she could have driven
that car home routinely,

00:12:46.595 --> 00:12:50.099
but she decided that that day
would not be like the others.

00:12:50.641 --> 00:12:53.311
She threw out that rule book
for just a second.

00:12:54.603 --> 00:12:56.605
I carry this lesson with me,

00:12:56.605 --> 00:12:59.483
that every day has within it hundreds,

00:12:59.525 --> 00:13:03.988
thousands of opportunities
to create real magic.

00:13:05.031 --> 00:13:07.199
Only, they disappear.

00:13:08.159 --> 00:13:10.703
Unless you reach out and grab them.

00:13:11.537 --> 00:13:12.663
Thank you.

00:13:12.705 --> 00:13:17.501
(Applause)

